{"text": " After a bad night, the worst thing you can do is sleep in. Here's how to get through the next day instead, and it starts the moment your alarm goes off. Say you slept 4 hours, so you sleep in until 11, nap for 2 hours after lunch and go to bed early to catch up, it feels smart, but at 1 in the morning you're wide awake again, one bad night just became 2. So here's what works step 1, get up at your usual time anyway, 7.30 same as always, you'll feel rough for an hour and that's ok. Step 2, get daylight in the first 10 minutes, open the curtains or take your coffee outside. Step 3, if you need a nap keep it short, 20 minutes before 3 in the afternoon with an alarm. And tonight, should you go to bed early? No, go to bed at your normal time, 11.30 not 9, by then you're tired enough to actually fall asleep. Do that and the bad night stays one bad night, you're asleep by midnight and the next morning feels normal again. Here are the 3 rules up at your usual time, a 20 minute nap before 3 your normal bedtime, send this to the friend who texted you at 4 am last night. That's what demure is for, you archive your dream today so you can see it tomorrow and break it down into small steps you can actually do. And follow because this afternoon's reel is the 5 minute check that finds money you're losing every month. If you want to try it out, I've put the link in the bio.", "segments": [{"id": 0, "seek": 0, "start": 0.0, "end": 4.52, "text": " After a bad night, the worst thing you can do is sleep in. Here's how to get through the next day", "tokens": [50363, 2293, 257, 2089, 1755, 11, 262, 5290, 1517, 345, 460, 466, 318, 3993, 287, 13, 3423, 338, 703, 284, 651, 832, 262, 1306, 1110, 50587], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.08627424818096739, "compression_ratio": 1.625, "no_speech_prob": 0.0007522498490288854, "words": [{"word": " After", "start": 0.0, "end": 0.38, "probability": 0.9204264283180237}, {"word": " a", "start": 0.38, "end": 0.52, "probability": 0.99473